From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Tue, 20 Sep 1994 16:16:02 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-327] comprehension gap?

Adam writes: 
> how do extropians feel about the effects of technology outrunning the
> capability of human comprehension?  

Technology has never been within the capability of any single
human's comprehension.  The gap is simply widening, slowly.
Modern software, often cited as an example of technology moving
beyond understanding, still has to be understood by a class of 
"maintainers" to a high degree of detail.  But in an 
increasing number of cases, details are hidden and even people
using a technology every day don't understand functions it performs
that are important to them.  For example, try asking a grocery
clerk if they are keeping databases of names and purchases of
people who use bank cards to pay.  The ones I've asked 
have been left in the dark on the matter.

