From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Tue, 20 Sep 1994 18:12:40 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-329] SCI: role for Q.M in neurobiology

pavel@PARK.bu.edu (Paul Cisek) writes on the subject of Penrose's
discussion of quantum mechanics and brain function:

X-Message-Number: #94-9-326
>However, the problem with the
>theory is that it still offers no functional motivation for the evolution of
>such a mechanism.  What non-computational problems did primitive organisms have
>to solve in order to survive?

I'd like to emphasize the point that there are advantages to be afforded by
quantum mechanics-based neurobiological mechanisms that do *not* have to do
with non-computability.  I would ephasize these two:

1) All of the proposals that I have heard about suggest information storage
densities and processing rates many orders of magnitude higher than what
would be estimated from a simple neural network model of neurobiological
function.

2) Recent research on "quantum computation" suggests that quantum mechanics
may simply be *qualitatively* better at solving the same sorts of problems
that can *also* be solved with classical computation... for example, Shor's
results on factoring in polynomial time with quantum computers.  This has
nothing to do with the issue of non-computability.

Wearing my biologist's hat, I'd like to point out that evolution doesn't
care about whether Q.M. type mechanisms are necessary, or fundamentally
better than classical mechanisms, anyway... evolution takes advantage of
whatever is possible and accessible through the fitness landscape.  The
mere fact that we can *imagine* some plausible specific quantum mechanical
processes, suggests that we take the possibility seriously.

Wearing my "Eastern Religions" hat, I'd like to point out the possibility
that such mechanisms might exist in all eukaryotic life forms, even
unicellular ones such as yeast, since some of them rely on fundamental
elements of cell structure shared by all eukaryotic cells (e.g.
microtubules).  Perhaps, then, we may discover that a scientific definition
of consciouness extends continuously to even unicellular life forms!

