Subject: [#94-9-332] TERM: "acracy", "acrat", "acratic"

At 03:51 PM 9/20/94 -0700, sasha1@netcom.com (Alexander Chislenko) wrote:
>  I agree with Dave Krieger that 'anarchy' in the sense we use this word
>is different from the well-known (and unpopular) concept of anarchy as
>absense of order (which is what it means etymologically).

Au contraire, etymologically it means without a *ruler* but has been 
perverted to mean "without law or order". The struggle to regain this
term reminds me of the network controversy about the word "hacker", and
it has been the subject of endless controversy in (left) anarchist
circles since before I was a pup. Back when I was a leftist -- since
I thought that was the only opposition -- we tried to rescue "anarchism"
to mean the system and let "anarchy" be used either way; the latest
groundswell I saw was the attempt by punk anarchists to retake the 
word "anarchy" and damn the connotations. 

>
>  This is not the meaning we want to convey.
>
>  'Acracy' seems to indicate absense of [any] governance; it sounds a bit
>better, though I would argue that
> 
>  1) governance doesn't necessarily indicate the presense of a State
>      or coersion  
>  2) in application to social systems, there is hardly any difference
>     between governance and order.
>
> I would like to see a term that would mean absense of coersion in general,
>by any social structure that may try to influence/restrict my life against
>my will.
>
>I am equally unhappy about being coersed by a territorial entity (State),
>ideological organization (Church, Party), mafia, or any other social group.
>So State is not the point here.
>
>Probably, the term we are looking for should have a familiar form of 'A...CY'
>where '...' is to be replaced by some euphonic synonym of 'coersion' or 'force'
>from one of the traditional sources of etymo-borrowings.
>
>If not the silly [i.e. semantically irrelevant]  linguo/phonic considerations,
>ACOERSY would suit me just fine.

It's funny (haha) but the "silly" etymology of "acracy" is exactly what
you want: a- for anti, absence of, and -cracy from kratos, force or
strength. I would think that having a literal origin of "without force"
is not much of an argument *for* the term, since it connotes "weak".

The exact term we want, keeping to the Greek roots, is "autocracy" for
self-rule, but that has already been co-opted to mean despotism, or rule
of many by one. Strange ... they didn't do the same to "autoeroticism".
It's as if someone were to maintain that "self-control" means one person
controls everyone.

OTOH, I like the assonance with "accuracy" and "accurate". And Sasha's
dismissal of linguistics is premature, because a term needs to "echo"
correctly, and it's not just scholars who can hear dissonances.

OTOOH, scholars who are too picky, who deride terms that mix Latin and
Greek roots (I can't remember an example offhand) would do well to look
at the accepted name of a genus of dinosaurs discovered in N. Africa --
_Oranosaurus_, where Ourano- is from a *Tuareg* root.

Since we don't learn Latin and Greek in school anymore -- and too damned 
bad, too, since languages, mathematics, logic and rhetoric are still a fine
curriculum -- we'd do well to coin chauvinistic Anglo terms like
"self-government" instead.

Conclusion? I dunno. I like "acracy" but I don't fancy having to explain
it without reference to "anarchy".



