  Well, I'm not sure on the specifics of the diff equation, but
obviously, it is the correlations which travel "instantaneously", not
a "disturbance" as a disturbance could be detected. Why is this
mind blowing? Classically, you can create a correlation which travels
faster than the speed of light - take a huge pair of scissors 
or laser beams sweeping the sky, the point of intersection between
the blades/beams can travel almost instantaneously.

   The strange thing in QM is that two particles can stay correlated
over huge distances. However, since the measurements yield
essentially random information, we can't use this for any
advantage. It's like flipping a coin and having the result
displayed simultaneously half way across the universe. You can't
control the coin's outcome.

   I'm still not convinced that QM is the "right theory" I'm hoping
something better comes along to explain some of the results
of QM.

